   144	## Project knowledge (this repo's loops practice)
   145	
   146	This monorepo **dogfoods** loops. Architectural choices, open arcs, frictions,
   147	and hypotheses accumulate in a local project vertex. Sessions read from it
   148	at start; emissions during work feed the next session's context.
   149	
   150	Two stores accumulate decisions, threads, tasks, hypotheses, and frictions
   135	The Store protocol is append-only: `append()`, `since(cursor)`, `between(start, end)`, `close()`. Three implementations:
   136	
   137	| Store | Backing | Use case |
   138	|-------|---------|----------|
   139	| `EventStore` | In-memory (optional JSONL) | Tests, ephemeral |
   140	| `SqliteStore` | SQLite (WAL mode) | sqlite-canonical stores |
   141	| `JsonlStore` | Append-only JSONL log + derived SQLite index | **Production** — the canonical shape |
   142	| `FileStore` | JSONL file | Legacy, pre-Receipt. Do not build on it. |
   143	
   144	**`JsonlStore` is a `SqliteStore` subclass, not an alternative to it.** The
   145	log is canonical; sqlite is a rebuildable index over it. All reads
   146	(`since`/`between`/`StoreReader`/FTS) are inherited byte-for-byte unchanged —
   147	only durability order is overridden: one flushed+fsynced log line, then the
   148	index row commits. Catch-up on open tails the log forward from a persisted
   149	byte offset, or rebuilds the index if the offset can't be trusted.
   150	
   151	**Which store a vertex gets is the store locator's extension**
   152	(`engine/residence.py` — the one place that answers "which file is
   153	authoritative"):
   154	
   155	```python
   156	from engine.residence import canonical_store_path, is_jsonl_canonical
   157	from engine.jsonl_store import ensure_index, open_canonical_store, resolved_index
   158	
   159	resolved_index(ast.store, vertex_path)       # → sqlite path to READ from
   160	canonical_store_path(ast.store, vertex_path) # → the authoritative artifact
   161	open_canonical_store(canonical, **kw)        # → JsonlStore or SqliteStore
   162	ensure_index(canonical)                      # → materialize a missing index
   163	```
   164	
   165	`resolved_index` is `residence.resolve_store_path` with materialization
   166	folded in. Resolve reads through it, never through the pure function: the
   167	pure one can only *name* a missing index, so a fresh clone's first read
   168	evaluates `exists()` before anything builds it and answers "empty store"
   169	once. **Writers resolve `canonical_store_path`, never an index path** — a
   170	`.db` looks identical whether it is canonical or derived, so a resolved
   171	index cannot answer the only question a writer may ask.
   172	
   173	`store "….jsonl"` → `JsonlStore` over the sibling `….db`; `store "….db"` →
   174	plain `SqliteStore`. Never construct a store for a vertex by hand — go
   175	through `open_canonical_store`, or a direct sqlite write becomes an
   176	out-of-band insert the log doesn't account for.
   177	
   178	Open-time detection of such writes is cheap by design and correspondingly
   179	narrow: stamped row counts vs `COUNT(*)` catch **inserts**, the last-line
   180	integrity compare catches an edit to the **last consumed** row, and an
   181	untrustworthy offset (outside `0..size`), a shrunk log or a torn tail trigger
   182	a rebuild. An in-place edit to an **interior** row opens clean — content
   183	verification is `verify_chain`'s job, since a tick's window hash commits to
