     1	"""jsonl_store — the canonical write path: JSONL is the store, sqlite an index.
     2	
     3	The authority flip of design/architecture/jsonl-canonical-store. Today a
     4	store's truth lives in sqlite; here the truth is ONE interleaved append-only
     5	log per store (``<name>.jsonl``, sibling of ``<name>.db``) and sqlite is a
     6	derived, rebuildable index over it.
     7	
     8	Seam choice (subclass, not wrapper)
     9	-----------------------------------
    10	``SqliteStore.append``/``append_tick`` do the mint work — id generation,
    11	chain linkage, window hashing, era-aware signing, the signing floor — and
    12	then persist one fully-assembled row. That row is *already* the exact shape
    13	the commitment hashers and :mod:`engine.jsonl_codec` take (7 fact fields /
    14	11 tick fields), so the only thing this layer must change is **where the row
    15	lands first**. A wrapper would have to re-derive or re-implement all of that
    16	mint logic to see the row; a subclass overriding the ``_write_fact_row`` /
    17	``_write_tick_row`` seam sees it for free, inherits every read method
    18	(``since``/``since_raw``/``between``/``StoreReader``/FTS) byte-for-byte
    19	unchanged, and adds no branch to the read path. Mint logic stays in one
    20	place; only durability order is overridden.
    21	
    22	Write order (the receipt originates at the log)
    23	-----------------------------------------------
    24	0. reconcile: if the stamped offset and the log's size disagree, run
    25	   catch-up before anything else. A post-fsync failure leaves a durable
    26	   line the index has not consumed; appending over it would stamp the
    27	   offset past the orphan and bury it while reporting ``synced``. No
    28	   append may ever skip an unindexed durable line;
    29	1. serialize the row through the S1 codec — ``payload`` rides as the
    30	   VERBATIM stored TEXT, never re-serialized, so every existing signature
    31	   and commitment hash survives the round trip;
    32	2. **stage** the sqlite INSERT in an open, uncommitted transaction. Nothing
    33	   is observable yet, but a rejected row (duplicate id via ``id_override``
    34	   on the transport/replay path, constraint violation) fails *here* — before
    35	   any byte reaches the log, so a refused write can never leave an orphan
    36	   line the index doesn't name;
    37	3. append the line + ``\\n`` to the log, ``flush()`` + ``os.fsync()`` — the
    38	   line is durable *before* the id is anything anyone can observe;
    39	4. stamp the consumed byte offset and row counts and COMMIT — the index row
    40	   becomes visible only after its line is durable, and row, offset and
    41	   counts can never disagree. Any failure rolls the whole transaction back,
    42	   leaving the log untouched;
    43	5. return the id. The id in the receipt is the id in the durable line.
    44	
    45	Staging the INSERT before the append inverts the literal "sqlite indexed
    46	after" ordering, but preserves the property that ordering exists for: an
    47	uncommitted row is invisible to every reader, and the commit lands strictly
    48	after the fsync. Crash between fsync and commit → the row is rolled back and
    49	the offset unstamped, so the next open tails the line forward.
    50	
    51	Catch-up on open
    52	----------------
    53	``store_meta.jsonl_offset`` holds the byte offset of the log consumed into
    54	sqlite. On open:
    55	
    56	- ``offset == size`` — in sync, nothing to do.
    57	- ``offset < size`` — sqlite is behind (crash between step 2 and step 3, or
    58	  an out-of-band append): tail forward, indexing the missed lines verbatim.
    59	- ``offset`` outside ``0..size`` (log shrank, or negative metadata), the
    60	  byte at ``offset - 1`` is not a
    61	  newline, or the last line ending at ``offset`` does not re-serialize to
    62	  the sqlite row it names — full rebuild of the sqlite index from the log,
    63	  logged loudly at WARNING. That last check is the "hash match": a
    64	  byte-compare of the codec's canonical line against the indexed row is
    65	  exactly a commitment comparison, done on one line rather than the file.
    66	- **offset absent** with a non-empty log: a store that has never recorded a
    67	  consumption point cannot claim any prefix is indexed, so it rebuilds
    68	  (rather than tailing from 0 into PK conflicts). This is also the shape a
    69	  freshly exported log + its source db has, and rebuild is the honest answer
    70	  there: it makes the index a pure function of the log.
    71	
    72	A torn final line (crash mid-write, no trailing newline) is **truncated**
    73	off the log before any tailing or appending — not merely skipped. Leaving
    74	partial bytes at the tail would let the next append concatenate onto junk
    75	and corrupt the canonical log. Truncation is the only mutation this layer
    76	ever performs on the log; everything else is append.
    77	
    78	Catch-up and rebuild insert rows **verbatim**: no signer runs, no floor
    79	check, no id minted, signatures ride as stored. Indexing is not re-emitting
    80	(same posture as ``store.jsonl.rebuild_jsonl``). A rebuild clears ``facts``
    81	and ``ticks`` only — ``store_meta`` (notably ``own_lineage``, the store's
    82	identity, which is not a fact) is preserved.
    83	
    84	This follows the :class:`engine.tailer.Tailer` pattern (persisted byte
    85	offset, complete-lines-only, incomplete tail left alone) but does not use
    86	the class: ``Tailer`` ``json.loads``-es each line before handing it on,
    87	discarding the verbatim line text this layer needs for the integrity
    88	compare and for the era-exact codec decode.
    89	
    90	Out-of-band sqlite writers
    91	--------------------------
    92	``libs/store``'s ``merge``/``receive``/``rebirth``/``compact`` open their own
    93	connection to the ``.db`` and write ``facts``/``ticks`` directly, bypassing
    94	the log entirely. This layer cannot refuse them (they never touch this
    95	class), so it *detects* them: the offset stamp carries the fact/tick counts
    96	consumed from the log, and open compares them against ``COUNT(*)``. A
    97	mismatch refuses with :class:`JsonlCanonicalUnsupported` rather than
    98	rebuilding — rebuilding would silently destroy exactly the rows that were
    99	written out of band.
   100	
   101	What open-time detection covers, exactly — it is two cheap checks, never a
   102	content walk, so the scope is narrower than "direct sqlite writes are
   103	detected":
   104	
   105	- **inserts** — the stamped counts vs ``COUNT(*)``;
   106	- an edit to the **last log-consumed row** — the last-line integrity
   107	  compare (:meth:`_prefix_intact`);
   108	- an offset outside ``0..size``, a shrunk log, a torn tail — rebuild
   109	  triggers.
   110	
   111	An in-place update to any **interior** row opens clean, and deliberately so:
   112	catching it would cost an O(n) walk of the whole log on every open. That
   113	walk is ``sl store verify``'s job — a fact sealed by a tick has its content
   114	committed in that tick's window hash, so ``verify_chain`` breaks on exactly
   115	the edit this path lets through. The residue is the custody boundary ticks
   116	already have: a **live-edge** fact (emitted, not yet sealed) is committed to
   117	by nothing, so a direct edit to it is undetectable until the next boundary —
   118	the same limit :meth:`SqliteStore.verify_facts` documents for signature
   119	strips. Both halves are pinned in ``tests/test_jsonl_store.py``.
   120	
   121	The counts also do not survive a store that simultaneously trips a rebuild
   122	trigger — a cleared offset marker or a shrunk log makes the stamped counts
   123	exactly as untrustworthy as the offset, so the rebuild runs and out-of-band
   124	rows are lost with it. Checking counts *before* rebuilding would block
   125	legitimate recoveries; two abnormal events have to stack to reach that
   126	window.
   127	
   128	The counts are never cached per handle: every stamp derives them from the
   129	*committed* marker, read under the write lock the INSERT already took. Two
   130	open handles on one store (a daemon plus an ``sl emit``) would otherwise each
   131	stamp their own stale idea of the total, and a perfectly consistent store
   132	would refuse to open, naming writers that never ran.
   133	
   134	Not yet wired (loud, not silent)
   135	--------------------------------
   136	History-mutating ops would rewrite rows sqlite-side while the log kept the
   137	originals, making the index no longer a function of the log. Until the log
   138	rewrite ceremony is designed they refuse with :class:`JsonlCanonicalUnsupported`:
   139	``absorb_edit`` and ``reanchor`` here; ``rebirth``/``compact`` live in
   140	``libs/store`` and are detected, not refused (above). ``absorb_genesis`` refuses
   141	too — the judgment call the slice allowed. It is append-shaped, not
   142	history-mutating, but its write is a ``BEGIN IMMEDIATE`` compare-and-swap
   143	that may roll back *after* the row is built; flush-first durability would
   144	make a rolled-back genesis real in the log. Reconciling the two orderings is
   145	design work, not a small wiring, so it fails loudly here.
   146	"""
   147	
   148	from __future__ import annotations
   149	
   150	import logging

codex
The main r1 scenarios are represented by honest regression tests and the implementations match their intended mechanics. One additional crash-window variant is not covered by those tests: a durable-but-unindexed tick followed immediately by another tick. Because tick linkage is minted before `_write()` reconciles, I’m testing that separately; it is the highest-risk edge in the remediation.
