b310e71 perf(loops): absorb-edit holds one store handle, not two
The ceremony opened the canonical store for the CAS token, closed it, and
opened it again to absorb. The token still comes first — that ordering is
what makes a concurrent edit refuse StaleDeclarationHead rather than
interleave — but the token carries the CAS, not the handle's lifetime, so
one handle spans both and a try/finally owns the close.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

4f073ea refactor(loops): resolvers compose; a constructor refusal renders as a refusal
- commands/store.resolve_store_path becomes ensure_index(resolve_canonical_path(…)),
  and commands/resolve._resolve_vertex_store_path becomes ensure_index over
  _resolve_vertex_canonical_store_path — deleting a second open-coded combine
  recursion. Which constituent of a combine is the writable one must have
  exactly one definition; two that can disagree is how a reader and a writer
  end up on different files.
- _run_store gains a JsonlCanonicalUnsupported backstop over the dispatch,
  rendering through a module-level _refuse_store (exit 2, the code every
  refusing path here already used). The nested per-command _refuse closures
  become partial() bindings of it, and reanchor's bespoke paint+return-2 is
  the same call. adopt on a poisoned store printed a raw traceback — the
  raise comes from JsonlStore.__init__, before any of adopt's own code —
  and now says what is wrong with the store; pinned by a test.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

022075d fix(engine,loops): five store-locator resolutions still spelled the old idiom
declaration.py (status, lineage/head, pin-check) and ls.py (kind stat,
vertex stat) each still joined the declared locator to the vertex dir by
hand. Under a .jsonl locator that hands the LOG path to a sqlite open,
which fails soft — the vertex reports no declaration and no facts rather
than erroring. declaration takes residence.resolve_store_path (pure — it
must not import jsonl_store); ls, at the app layer, takes resolved_index
so a fresh clone materializes.

Regression tests pinned in both places; each fails against the idiom it
replaces.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

3788424 refactor(engine): store_meta through one accessor pair; log naming in residence
- _meta_get/_meta_set land beside _ensure_meta_table on SqliteStore, and
  jsonl_store's six inline SELECT/INSERT OR REPLACE spellings go through
  them. _meta_set stages without committing: every meta write here is part
  of a larger atomic step. The offset-int parse is shared as _as_int, so
  _index_is_current and _read_meta_int answer the same way.
- _index_is_current reuses declaration._open_readonly instead of its own
  read-only connect, at timeout=0.25 (not the 5s default) — it runs on
  every read resolve, and lock contention is a fine reason to answer 'not
  current' and let catch-up decide.
- log_path_for moves to residence beside index_path_for: both directions
  of the .db<->.jsonl bijection in the module that owns the naming rule.
  residence's docstring no longer points back out at jsonl_store.
- absorb_genesis's fact INSERT was a fourth spelling — it takes
  FACT_INSERT_SQL.
- _append_line returns fh.tell() instead of re-stat'ing the log.
- emit_topology's resolved_index result was assigned and never read.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

82b79ae refactor(engine,store): one spelling of the persisted column order
The fact/tick INSERT was written three times and the column order four —
jsonl_store's module constants, sqlite_store's inline _write_*_row SQL,
store/jsonl.py's _FACT_COLS, and jsonl_store._row_matches' SELECT list.
sqlite_store owns the schema and the _write_*_row seam, so it states the
columns once (derived from the codec's field tuples) and builds
FACT_INSERT_SQL/TICK_INSERT_SQL from them; jsonl_store and libs/store
import. _TICK_ROW_SQL and its V1 variant join the same tuples.

rebuild_jsonl was a second implementation of the rebuild engine already
owns — the same read-the-log, insert-verbatim walk JsonlStore runs on an
untrustworthy offset. It now constructs a JsonlStore at the target over
the source log and keeps only what is migration-side: the never-overwrite
guard and the counts. The rebuilt index comes out stamped rather than
rebuild-on-reopen; the oracle round-trip is unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

c4d34f3 refactor(engine): one codec spec table, keyed by the discriminator
serialize/deserialize each spelled the fact and tick rules a second time —
four call sites threading (fields, allowed, nullable, t) as loose
positionals. One module-level _SPEC keyed by "t" holds them; _encode and
deserialize_row are the only two code paths, so a rule added for one
record type cannot be forgotten for the other. Field tuples become public
(FACT_FIELDS/TICK_FIELDS) so the schema can derive from them next.

deserialize_fact_row/deserialize_tick_row had zero production callers —
dropped from engine's public surface, their tests retargeted at
deserialize_row with a discriminator assert.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

5dfc081 refactor(loops): one change-fact emitter, not two byte-identical copies
add.py and rm.py each carried the same ~45-line _maybe_emit_change —
canonical-locator resolution, observer, the never-fail-the-mutation
comment block, all of it. It lives once in commands/resolve.py as
emit_change_fact, beside the resolution it turns on; both verbs keep
their private name as a delegate.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>


